# Jira ID Compliance for Branches and Commits

## Context
<!-- Context to be defined: Explain when and why this rule applies. -->

## Rules

- Every new commit must be associated with a valid **Jira Issue Key** (`HAT-159`, `ABC-1234`, etc.).
- One of the following must be true:
  - The **branch name** must start with a Jira ID in the format `[A-Z]{2,10}-[0-9]+`, followed by a short description.
    - Example: `HAT-159-fix-query-issue`
  - **OR** the **commit title** must start with the Jira ID, followed by a vertical bar (`|`), then the short commit message.
    - Example: `HAT-159 | fix query issue`

- Prefer having the Jira ID **in both** the branch name **and** the commit title if possible for maximum traceability.

## Jira Key Format

- Must be:
  - 2-10 uppercase letters (A-Z)
  - A dash `-`
  - 1-6 digits
- Examples:
  - `HAT-159`
  - `API-3401`
  - `AUTH-78`

## Commit Title Structure
- Example: `HAT-159 | Add missing indexes for query optimization`
- Use imperative mood ("Add", "Fix", "Update"), not past tense ("Added", "Fixed", "Updated").

## Bad Examples

<example type="invalid">
- Branch name: `fix-query` (❌ Missing Jira ID)
- Commit title: `fixed queries` (❌ Missing Jira ID and passive tense)
- Branch: `hotfix-urgent`, commit: `quick fix` (❌ Missing traceability)
</example>

## Good Examples

<example>
- Branch: `HAT-159-optimize-database-index`
- Commit Title: `HAT-159 | Optimize database index lookup`

OR

- Branch: `feature/add-new-endpoints`
- Commit Title: `HAT-159 | Add new REST API endpoints`
</example>

## Enforcement Notes

- If the branch name includes a Jira ID correctly, commit title can optionally be shorter.
- If the branch name does **not** contain a Jira ID, commit title **must** include it.
- Always aim for clean traceability back to project management (Jira).

# Notes

- This rule ensures traceability from code → commit → Jira ticket → feature/release.
- Non-compliance requires commit title reformatting or branch rename before merge or delivery.
